WEBVTT

00:00:23.891 --> 00:00:25.425
[horn honks]

00:00:28.367 --> 00:00:33.667
[Jerry] <i>This is a 1964 Morgan Plus 4,</i>
<i>in brilliant blue with a black interior.</i>

00:00:34.167 --> 00:00:36.533
<i>It has a four-speed manual gear box...</i>

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:37.633
[engine revs]

00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:41.967
<i>...and dual carbs</i>
<i>feeding a 105-horsepower motor.</i>

00:00:43.267 --> 00:00:45.100
<i>Morgans are unbelievable cars.</i>

00:00:45.233 --> 00:00:47.500
<i>They're very British. </i>
<i>Brits like tradition.</i>

00:00:47.567 --> 00:00:51.200
<i>This basic design has been</i>
<i>in production since 1936.</i>

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:55.233
<i>The frame is actually made of ash wood,</i>
<i>which makes no sense.</i>

00:00:55.367 --> 00:00:56.800
<i>Wood is not for cars.</i>

00:00:56.867 --> 00:00:58.833
<i>It's for ventriloquist dummy heads.</i>

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.900
<i>These cars are so cool to me</i>
<i>because they just don't care</i>

00:01:02.967 --> 00:01:04.733
<i>what the other companies are doing.</i>

00:01:07.401 --> 00:01:10.370
<i>A lot like my very special guest today,</i>
<i>Mr. Stephen Colbert...</i>

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.100
<i>former host of</i> The Colbert Report,

00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:16.200
<i>and has recently been picked by CBS</i>
<i>to replace David Letterman</i>

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:18.333
<i>and be their late-night talk show star.</i>

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:21.433
You never told me it was fun!

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:24.767
[Jerry] <i>Stephen also looks like</i>
<i>a very conventional, functional person</i>

00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:27.233
<i>but inside his head, he's a madman.</i>

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:29.267
-Oh, my God.
-Can you dig it?

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.767
[phone ringing]

00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:32.600
<i>-</i>[Stephen] <i>Hello?</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Stephen?</i>

00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:34.795
<i>-</i>[Stephen] <i>Jerry?</i>
<i>-</i>[Jerry]<i> Are your children at home?</i>

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:36.200
[Stephen] <i>No, they're not.</i>

00:01:36.267 --> 00:01:38.933
<i>That's an odd lead to a conversation,</i>
<i>I have to say, though.</i>

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:40.333
[Jerry laughs]

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.867
[Jerry] <i>I wanted to have breakfast</i>
<i>with somebody amusing, someone witty.</i>

00:01:43.971 --> 00:01:45.205
[Stephen]<i> I'm out of practice.</i>

00:01:45.272 --> 00:01:47.741
<i>I haven't had a television show</i>
<i>for almost five months.</i>

00:01:47.808 --> 00:01:49.776
[Jerry] <i>Come and see yourself </i>
<i>as a sports car.</i>

00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:58.100
<i>I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is</i>
Comedians In Cars Getting Coffee.

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:06.893
-[dog barking]
-Hey.

00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:11.100
Scratch, now lick. There it is.

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:14.968
[Stephen] Wow...

00:02:15.067 --> 00:02:16.967
-Is this original?
-[Jerry] Yeah.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.267
It's wood frame,

00:02:18.333 --> 00:02:21.667
so you don't have to worry about rust,
but you do have to worry about termites.

00:02:21.833 --> 00:02:23.167
[Stephen] It's gorgeous.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.213
I want to ask you for a stick of Beemans
before I strap in here.

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.949
-I think my beard even matches this.
-[Jerry laughs]

00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:31.567
-Whose car is that, the wife?
-[Stephen] That is my daughter's.

00:02:31.652 --> 00:02:33.320
-Your daughter?
-Soon to be my son's.

00:02:33.387 --> 00:02:35.255
-Sex change?
-That's...

00:02:36.833 --> 00:02:37.833
We don't know.

00:02:37.967 --> 00:02:40.967
Is it just a matter of time
before transgender is an airline?

00:02:41.061 --> 00:02:44.297
You just get on one sex
and you get off, you're the other one.

00:02:49.433 --> 00:02:51.733
[Stephen]
It really has that airplane feel to it.

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:54.967
I feel like we're about to fly across
the English Channel

00:02:55.033 --> 00:02:57.000
to save the people stranded in Dover.

00:02:57.133 --> 00:03:00.067
Perhaps someday we could rent
a cottage on the Isle of Wight.

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.582
-If it's not too dear. Yeah.
-If it's not too dear. Exactly.

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.455
[Jerry] Turn signals,
you're gonna love this.

00:03:08.522 --> 00:03:09.589
[clicking]

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:11.767
Here's my horn.

00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:12.767
[horn honking]

00:03:14.033 --> 00:03:16.933
Would not help you in any way.

00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:18.667
"We're British, we don't need help.

00:03:18.733 --> 00:03:20.800
-We're just fine as we are."
-Everything...

00:03:23.937 --> 00:03:26.006
Take out the little book, too,
I love the book.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:29.367
Does it have, like,
a section on etiquette in here?

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:31.367
"Do not interrupt the driver mid-shift."

00:03:31.433 --> 00:03:33.500
-How adorable. It's adorable.
-Fantastic.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.233
A little mouse should live in there.

00:03:35.367 --> 00:03:37.133
[giggling]

00:03:37.484 --> 00:03:39.720
A little mouse,
his name is Wilfred, he goes...

00:03:39.786 --> 00:03:41.555
"Can I get master anything?"

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:48.333
-[Stephen] Is this a '64?
-[Jerry] '64.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.267
-I was born in '64.
-No.

00:03:50.333 --> 00:03:53.667
Yeah. I love how dangerous this feels.

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:54.933
Watch this.

00:03:56.503 --> 00:03:57.771
[Stephen laughing]

00:03:58.967 --> 00:04:01.867
-Is that the most precious...
-And open up this,

00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:04.000
and Wilfred is on a little bike.

00:04:04.167 --> 00:04:05.333
[both laughing]

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:07.647
On a chain, making them move.

00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:10.517
"I wasn't aware that it was spinning,
master!"

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:17.400
I started off thinking
I was gonna be an actor,

00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:20.033
and then got hired by Second City,
and did sketch comedy,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:21.467
and cabaret for years.

00:04:21.967 --> 00:04:24.933
I started <i>The Daily Show</i> on June 2, 1997.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.567
But that was Craig Kilborn years.
And then I left.

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:29.933
Though I came back in 2000
for the election,

00:04:30.233 --> 00:04:34.267
and there was this new guy hosting,
Jon Stewart, who my wife knew, actually.

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.033
She always lords it over me that
she's known Jon longer than I have.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:40.100
I think his roommate dated her roommate,
or something like that.

00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:41.567
-Right.
-When he got the gig,

00:04:41.615 --> 00:04:44.484
she was like, "That's Jon Leibowitz,
what's he doing? He's not funny."

00:04:50.233 --> 00:04:53.933
You're one of the greatest
television performers I have ever seen.

00:04:54.567 --> 00:04:56.600
-Can I get out here?
-I'm just telling you that.

00:04:56.667 --> 00:04:59.467
-That's very nice of you to say.
-You really are. On TV.

00:04:59.967 --> 00:05:01.300
You had to do that.

00:05:01.533 --> 00:05:03.133
You had to put "on TV."

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.767
Well, I've never seen...
You're not a stage performer.

00:05:05.806 --> 00:05:08.041
-I was at a certain point.
-But I never saw any of that.

00:05:08.108 --> 00:05:09.709
-Telling you what I saw.
-No one will.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:11.700
There's a lot of people on TV, Stephen.

00:05:11.767 --> 00:05:13.567
-A lot.
-You're very nice.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:15.482
[Jerry] What you did with that character,

00:05:15.549 --> 00:05:17.918
and the energy and the fun,

00:05:17.984 --> 00:05:20.720
-you never seemed tired.
-I was ready to stop.

00:05:20.867 --> 00:05:22.067
-You were?
-I was gonna stop

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.567
-whether or not...
-Really?

00:05:23.623 --> 00:05:25.292
Yeah, the Letterman thing fell in my lap.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.894
Why'd you stop your show?
Your show was a very good show, Jerry.

00:05:27.967 --> 00:05:31.867
You're one of the greatest performers
I've ever seen on television. On NBC.

00:05:32.032 --> 00:05:34.334
One of the greatest performers
I've ever seen on NBC.

00:05:34.434 --> 00:05:36.102
[door handle clicking]

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:39.100
-Let me reach over there...
-Not only a death trap...

00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:40.900
-A suicide trap.
-Yep.

00:05:40.967 --> 00:05:42.367
We better get out while we're young.

00:05:42.542 --> 00:05:45.412
[inaudible conversation]

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:51.267
What time would you get home at night?

00:05:51.333 --> 00:05:53.833
For the first couple of years,
it was like I lived there.

00:05:53.967 --> 00:05:55.833
I tried to be, like, the first guy in,

00:05:55.889 --> 00:05:58.291
and then I wouldn't leave
till 11:00 at night.

00:05:58.433 --> 00:05:59.433
Wow.

00:05:59.667 --> 00:06:01.500
Yeah, 'cause we didn't know
what we were doing.

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:10.503
Can we start with some coffee?

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:11.471
-Sure.
-Thank you.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.733
I saw your beard recently, and I thought,

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:22.633
"If he hasn't shaved,
this is gonna be gruesome."

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.067
But I see that you're...

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:26.100
I'm going for a mid-career Connery.

00:06:26.367 --> 00:06:28.800
-A "mid-Connery," we call it.
-A mid-Connery.

00:06:32.167 --> 00:06:34.100
<i>When I was in Chicago</i>
<i>and I'd be auditioning,</i>

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:36.500
-never got anything, by the way.
-Really?

00:06:36.567 --> 00:06:39.233
I think I got one Iowa Bank commercial.

00:06:39.333 --> 00:06:41.900
So remember these three words
for banking convenience.

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:43.567
"You're first here..."

00:06:43.833 --> 00:06:44.867
...at FirsTier.

00:06:44.905 --> 00:06:46.506
[Jerry laughs]

00:06:46.573 --> 00:06:49.042
I like the way you adjust your glasses.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.500
What did I do?

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:52.400
Yes. It's refined.

00:06:52.467 --> 00:06:54.833
It's like cleaning your pipe.
If you had a pipe,

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.200
you could do this, you go, "Well, Jerry...

00:06:58.833 --> 00:07:00.433
Jerry, let me..."

00:07:10.333 --> 00:07:13.333
Come on, let's be honest,
a pipe and a beard is gross.

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:15.567
-It's just gross.
-What, 'cause it lingers?

00:07:15.633 --> 00:07:18.133
Just, everything is hanging and yellow,

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.567
the cheese, the eggs, it's all yellow.

00:07:20.633 --> 00:07:23.333
He's growing beards, he's packing bowls.

00:07:24.433 --> 00:07:27.767
The cheese. I don't think
I'm gonna have a good day today.

00:07:28.167 --> 00:07:30.300
Oh, my God. Oh, my God.

00:07:32.852 --> 00:07:34.387
Have you ever smoked a pipe?

00:07:34.521 --> 00:07:37.490
Uh, no, but I own several nice pipes.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:40.267
I can't believe you could
do that pipe thing

00:07:40.333 --> 00:07:43.300
without being a pipe smoker.
You did that so well.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:44.800
I'm sure you have talents.

00:07:44.867 --> 00:07:46.367
This is my biggest one.

00:07:46.433 --> 00:07:49.402
-Noticing? Noticing?
-Noticing what the hell's going on.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:52.605
That's very nice. My dad smoked a pipe.
My dad smoked a pipe...

00:07:52.700 --> 00:07:55.333
-That's where you got it from.
-He ran a hospital.

00:07:55.375 --> 00:07:57.677
-Ever see <i>Hospital </i>with George C. Scott?
-Of course.

00:07:57.811 --> 00:08:00.613
I would like to talk
about George C. Scott.

00:08:00.700 --> 00:08:01.700
George C. Scott.

00:08:01.767 --> 00:08:03.633
-Are we ever...
<i>-The Flim-Flam Man.</i>

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:05.267
-...gonna see...
<i>-Patton.</i>

00:08:05.333 --> 00:08:07.267
...those guys again?

00:08:07.787 --> 00:08:10.857
I did a show at Second City once, where
we were doing a "men's movement" scene,

00:08:10.933 --> 00:08:14.367
where we're all getting in touch
with our primal masculinity.

00:08:14.533 --> 00:08:16.867
Five guys are in the scene,
we all started chanting,

00:08:17.000 --> 00:08:19.733
"George C. Scott. George C. Scott."

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:22.333
That was the most masculine thing
we could say.

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:28.000
Steve McQueen has become a symbol
of what men used to be that we miss.

00:08:28.067 --> 00:08:30.767
It would be wonderful to find out
that Steve McQueen was gay, though.

00:08:30.833 --> 00:08:33.300
Him and Charles Bronson.
Yeah, you... Come on.

00:08:33.367 --> 00:08:35.500
-Come on. Are you kidding?
-Come on, wake up.

00:08:35.567 --> 00:08:37.100
Morning, noon, and night.

00:08:37.233 --> 00:08:40.300
He was running around with Rip Taylor,
Paul Lynde, Gomer Pyle.

00:08:40.367 --> 00:08:42.300
They had a poker game every Tuesday night.

00:08:42.367 --> 00:08:44.933
-Yep.
-Yeah, they're all gay. Everyone's gay.

00:08:49.496 --> 00:08:52.365
Not to belabor, though there's nothing
I love more than to belabor.

00:08:52.433 --> 00:08:55.367
You saw guys like George C. Scott and go,
"I want to be like that."

00:08:55.433 --> 00:08:57.800
Right. Well, also,
it goes with the astronaut core, too.

00:08:57.837 --> 00:08:59.072
Laying it out over the line.

00:08:59.139 --> 00:09:00.840
That's why I said
when I got in your car,

00:09:00.907 --> 00:09:02.208
"You got a stick of Beemans?"

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.533
Chuck Yeager, the Beemans,
<i>The Right Stuff.</i>

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:07.300
At the end of that movie,
the last line of it is,

00:09:07.367 --> 00:09:10.900
they're driving up on his wreck,
when he's chasing that demon,

00:09:10.967 --> 00:09:13.633
and they're looking at the smoke,
and here comes Sam Shepard,

00:09:13.700 --> 00:09:15.767
cut up and bloody, but looking good,

00:09:15.867 --> 00:09:18.333
and the ambulance driver goes,
"Is that a man?"

00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.200
And he goes, "Yes, it is."

00:09:20.333 --> 00:09:23.133
In other words,
risk-taking and adventurous and...

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:26.733
But what do you think
the missing factor is?

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:29.669
It's "the hell with it."
What got you into this business.

00:09:29.736 --> 00:09:30.837
"The hell with it."

00:09:30.904 --> 00:09:32.839
-I have no other skills.
-If you think about a...

00:09:32.906 --> 00:09:35.508
You could've acquired them.
You acquired this.

00:09:36.800 --> 00:09:40.167
What I'm saying to you is,
women are becoming more masculine,

00:09:40.233 --> 00:09:41.867
men are becoming more feminine.

00:09:41.933 --> 00:09:44.200
Who's more feminine, you or your father?

00:09:44.533 --> 00:09:45.933
Obviously I am.

00:09:47.954 --> 00:09:51.991
Because he actually smoked,
and I pretend to smoke

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:53.467
with a spoon.

00:10:00.467 --> 00:10:02.100
How do you feel about happiness?

00:10:02.167 --> 00:10:03.600
Where do you rank happiness?

00:10:03.667 --> 00:10:05.867
I think it's a foolish thing to pursue.

00:10:05.933 --> 00:10:08.433
Suffering is actually
a pretty good way to get to happiness.

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.800
Exactly. Like I'm suffering right now,
trying to make you happy.

00:10:12.833 --> 00:10:14.933
You have. You've made me happy, Jerry.

00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:17.067
One of the first gigs I got
when I was out of college

00:10:17.133 --> 00:10:18.900
was, I did a children's theater show.

00:10:18.967 --> 00:10:21.800
The woman who ran the program
was going through a divorce.

00:10:21.933 --> 00:10:23.333
She said, "What should I do?"

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.000
She laid out the different options
she had, how to handle her relationship.

00:10:27.167 --> 00:10:32.000
And I said, "You should think of the thing
that you least want to do.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:35.133
That's probably the right thing to do.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.133
And you know it.
Which is why you don't want to do it."

00:10:38.338 --> 00:10:42.041
And she just looked at me, she goes,
"Are you sure you're not Jewish?"

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.946
And I took it as a compliment.
I'm like, "Yeah, that's nice."

00:10:51.784 --> 00:10:53.820
-You're obviously...
-Existing is a complete...

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:55.533
What a trial.

00:10:55.633 --> 00:10:59.667
One of my favorite Richard Pryor lines,
his bit about racism, he says,

00:10:59.726 --> 00:11:03.296
"You know, forget about racism.
It's hard enough just being a person."

00:11:03.367 --> 00:11:05.367
Do you know the band Neutral Milk Hotel?

00:11:05.467 --> 00:11:06.633
Is that an antacid?

00:11:08.333 --> 00:11:10.167
He's got a song, and one of the lines is,

00:11:10.267 --> 00:11:12.533
"And someday, we will die
And our ashes will fly

00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:14.333
From the airplane over the sea

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:17.800
And when we meet on a cloud
I'll be laughing out loud

00:11:17.867 --> 00:11:20.133
I'll be laughing with everyone I see

00:11:20.467 --> 00:11:23.533
Can't believe how strange it is
to be anything at all"

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:27.167
And I really love that idea
of how strange it is to be...

00:11:27.533 --> 00:11:28.633
Okay, Jerry.

00:11:31.567 --> 00:11:32.567
Too much.

00:11:35.867 --> 00:11:38.667
What's the last thing you want to taste
right before you die?

00:11:38.767 --> 00:11:41.067
Arctic frost blueberry Twizzlers.

00:11:42.933 --> 00:11:43.933
Maybe coffee.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:45.900
You really want to perk up
right at that moment?

00:11:45.967 --> 00:11:47.567
It picks you up while it calms you down.

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:49.833
And what's the last thing
you want to hear?

00:11:49.900 --> 00:11:51.633
Mendelssohn's <i>Song Without Words,</i>

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.433
which is short,
two-and-a-half minutes long or something.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:56.100
Good, 'cause we don't have a lot of time.

00:12:00.567 --> 00:12:04.467
Do you use jazz language at all?
Like "cat," "daddy-o," "dig it"?

00:12:04.533 --> 00:12:06.167
I won't say "dig it" or "daddy-o."

00:12:06.233 --> 00:12:07.800
-"Can you dig it?"
-"Can you dig it"?

00:12:07.900 --> 00:12:09.300
You'll say, "Can you dig it"?

00:12:09.367 --> 00:12:11.100
I can say "Dig it."

00:12:11.433 --> 00:12:13.333
Really? You got a lot of balls.

00:12:21.341 --> 00:12:22.875
Did you like <i>Whiplash?</i>

00:12:23.076 --> 00:12:25.378
-Uh, you know what?
-Oh, no.

00:12:26.212 --> 00:12:27.880
No, don't.

00:12:27.947 --> 00:12:30.350
-What do you think I'm gonna say?
-I know what you're gonna say.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.852
You had some problems with it
and I don't wanna hear it.

00:12:32.919 --> 00:12:35.355
I know people who play jazz.

00:12:35.433 --> 00:12:38.867
They said, "The idea that
that's how you get good at jazz

00:12:38.933 --> 00:12:41.133
is not how you get good at jazz."

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:42.533
-I know.
-And I said,

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:45.300
"I've got a feeling that's not
how you get good at boxing,

00:12:45.367 --> 00:12:46.733
but I like <i>Rocky."</i>

00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:56.133
What do you think
of the idea for this show?

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:57.433
Jerry.

00:12:57.767 --> 00:12:59.033
[Jerry laughs]

00:13:00.333 --> 00:13:01.667
How long do we get to do this?

00:13:01.733 --> 00:13:04.800
-Whatever you like. We can leave...
-No, no, this is very enjoyable.

00:13:04.867 --> 00:13:07.233
-I think the lulls are nice.
-I love the lulls.

00:13:07.333 --> 00:13:09.433
I can't talk as long as you can.

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.567
You can talk in paragraphs
and just keep going.

00:13:12.700 --> 00:13:14.833
Let's do a lull now,
because I'm exhausted.

00:13:21.167 --> 00:13:23.303
-One of the--
-That was enough of a lull for you?

00:13:25.872 --> 00:13:28.508
Did it get uncomfortable?
Do you have to fill the silences?

00:13:28.574 --> 00:13:31.110
This is much longer
than you'll be on my show.

00:13:31.177 --> 00:13:34.914
To do a guest appearance on your show,
it's no [bleep] fun.

00:13:35.148 --> 00:13:36.616
-This is relaxing.
-Everything...

00:13:36.683 --> 00:13:38.484
-This is very relaxing.
-Exactly.

00:13:38.551 --> 00:13:40.953
When I come on your show,
I'm gonna do a stand-up set.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:42.955
Know how long it's gonna take
to put that together?

00:13:43.022 --> 00:13:45.925
So, you cannot compare the pain in the ass

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:50.200
of doing your show
to the joy and pleasure of doing mine.

00:13:53.299 --> 00:13:55.301
[inaudible]

00:13:59.933 --> 00:14:02.100
[Jerry] Which would you rather be,
smart or funny?

00:14:02.167 --> 00:14:03.767
-I'd rather be funny.
-Me, too.

00:14:05.667 --> 00:14:09.500
So, you'd be a clown, then?
Just an idiot in gigantic pants.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:17.490
[Jerry] Wanna go in this bookstore?
You think you're so smart.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.100
[Stephen] When I was younger,
living in Chicago,

00:14:25.167 --> 00:14:28.000
the one thing I would never deny myself
was books.

00:14:28.067 --> 00:14:29.633
That's really pretentious.

00:14:34.367 --> 00:14:35.400
Could I drive?

00:14:35.467 --> 00:14:37.367
-You can drive a stick?
-Sure.

00:14:37.443 --> 00:14:40.480
-How great do you feel with those gloves?
-They feel really nice.

00:14:46.052 --> 00:14:47.653
[Jerry] The weird thing, of course,

00:14:47.733 --> 00:14:50.300
is that New York is now
the center of talk shows.

00:14:50.367 --> 00:14:52.233
-Isn't that interesting?
-That's the weird thing.

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:55.367
But both you and Jimmy Fallon
are adult enough

00:14:55.433 --> 00:14:56.933
to not get into any of that.

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:59.267
I think nothing would be more boring

00:14:59.333 --> 00:15:01.033
-than <i>Late Night</i> war.
-Yeah.

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.069
We had drinks and talked about it,
actually.

00:15:03.136 --> 00:15:04.670
-You and Jimmy Fallon?
-Both drunk.

00:15:04.737 --> 00:15:08.274
Yeah. It was shortly after it was
announced that I was gonna get the gig.

00:15:08.341 --> 00:15:10.510
I said, "Let's get together
and have some drinks,"

00:15:10.610 --> 00:15:12.478
and we laughed and told stories.

00:15:12.545 --> 00:15:15.081
That's it. We just laughed like this.
And it was lovely.

00:15:17.433 --> 00:15:19.667
My favorite thing of today
was George C. Scott.

00:15:19.733 --> 00:15:22.400
-George C. Scott.
-He's not coloring his hair.

00:15:22.467 --> 00:15:23.900
He doesn't give a [bleep].

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.033
-Why?
-Right?

00:15:25.091 --> 00:15:26.959
-You think I'm coloring my hair?
-Obviously.

00:15:27.026 --> 00:15:27.994
I'm not.

00:15:42.508 --> 00:15:44.477
-That was a pretty good lull, though.
-Yeah.

00:15:44.733 --> 00:15:46.800
[Jerry] That was...
Might even be our best lull.

00:15:46.867 --> 00:15:47.700
[Stephen] Yeah.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:53.033
[gear grinding]

00:15:53.085 --> 00:15:55.655
Oops! Yeah. Yeah, now you're
going into first.

00:15:55.721 --> 00:15:57.390
-You wanna go into third.
-There.

00:15:58.324 --> 00:16:00.593
-I haven't driven a stick in forever.
-Really?

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.728
Not that you can tell.
Here's why, is that I feel...

00:16:02.800 --> 00:16:04.167
-Oh, God.
-Sorry.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:06.933
-[gears grinding]
-No, no, no. Second... No, you...

00:16:06.999 --> 00:16:08.067
Wait, nothing at all?

00:16:08.134 --> 00:16:11.270
Oh, God, Stephen, no.
You don't know what you're doing.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:13.339
[engine racing]

00:16:15.367 --> 00:16:19.967
Absolutely horrible driving. Horrible.

00:16:20.179 --> 00:16:22.548
I'm not gonna get any action
at the end of this, am I?

00:16:22.648 --> 00:16:23.549
No.

00:16:24.133 --> 00:16:25.400
My kids aren't home.